 "tocNote": "Tokyo is split by neighbourhood; Osaka and Kyoto run by category. Categories always appear in this order: streetwear &amp; vintage, archive &amp; designer vintage, watches &amp; vintage hobby, jdm culture, gorpcore &amp; snow, underground music, electronic &amp; clubs, jazz bars, vinyl bars, beer &amp; whisky, izakaya, omakase, indian, board game bars, pool bars, golf &amp; driving range, onsen &amp; sento, after dark, brunch, vintage nintendo, maid cafes, gaming cafes, bowling alleys, hidden temples &amp; shrines, cool bars, good food. Tap a name for its website and tap ⌖ for the map. Small rooms change hours without notice, so check Instagram on the day.",
 "rules": {
  "title": "House rules — read once",
  "items": [
   "Carry cash. Many small rooms take nothing else; a few (noted) are cashless only.",
   "Live houses add a compulsory drink (“+1D”, about ¥600). Door price usually runs ¥300–500 above advance.",
   "Bars often add a table charge (otoshi at izakaya), and small counters may turn away groups of four or more.",
   "Reserve by email with your name, the date and how many people. Sold out usually means no door sales.",
   "Clubs check photo ID at the door; bring your passport.",
   "Many basements sit in residential streets. Don't hang around outside; it can cost the venue its licence.",
   "Upstairs and basement shops keep late, irregular hours. Most open around 1–2pm."
  ]
 },
